Consider a classic boat and streams problem where a boat traverses a river with a current. In such problems, the boat’s effective speed is different when moving upstream versus downstream because of the river’s current. For example, a boat moving in still water with speed v km/h is assisted by the current when going downstream (effective speed = v + u km/h) and hindered when going upstream (effective speed = v – u km/h), where u km/h is the speed of the stream. A common variant is: given the distance d traveled (which remains the same in each direction) along with the time taken downstream (t₁) and upstream (t₂), determine the boat’s speed in still water (v) and the stream’s speed (u).

Constraints:
– The distance d is a positive real number.
– The times t₁ and t₂ are positive and correspond to the downstream and upstream journeys respectively.
– The boat’s speed (v) must be greater than the stream’s speed (u) to ensure upstream motion is possible.

Section 2 — Intuition
The key insight is the concept of relative speed:

When moving downstream, the stream aids the boat so that the effective speed is (v + u).
When moving upstream, the current works against the boat making the effective speed (v – u).
Using the time–distance relationship (Distance = Speed × Time), if the boat covers the same distance d in both directions, we can form two equations and solve them simultaneously to derive formulas for v and u.
Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force (Iterative Search)
A brute force method would involve iterating over possible pairs of v and u (ensuring v > u) until both equations
  d = t₁ × (v + u) and
  d = t₂ × (v – u)
are nearly satisfied. Although this approach might work for small ranges or very specific inputs, it is not efficient or elegant.

Below is a commented C++ code snippet demonstrating the brute force method:

Copy
cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Given constants
    double d = 30.0;        // distance (km)
    double t_down = 3.0;    // time downstream (hours)
    double t_up = 5.0;      // time upstream (hours)
    
    // Tolerance for floating point comparison
    const double EPS = 1e-6;
    
    // Brute force search ranges
    double v_found = 0.0, u_found = 0.0;
    bool found = false;
    
    // Try possible v and u values with a reasonable step
    for (double v = 1.0; v <= 100.0; v += 0.01) {    // boat speed in still water
        for (double u = 0.0; u < v; u += 0.01) {       // stream speed must be less than v
            double downstream = v + u;                 
            double upstream   = v - u;                 
            if (fabs(d / downstream - t_down) < EPS &&
                fabs(d / upstream - t_up) < EPS) {
                v_found = v;
                u_found = u;
                found = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (found) break;
    }
    
    if(found) {
        cout << "Boat speed in still water (v): " << v_found << " km/h" << endl;
        cout << "Stream speed (u): " << u_found << " km/h" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "No solution found within the search range." << endl;
    }
    
    return 0;
}
Time Complexity: O(n²) due to the nested loops over v and u.
Space Complexity: O(1) because only a few variables are used.

Section 4 — Approach 2: Optimal Solution
An optimal solution uses algebra to derive the formulas directly without resorting to iterative searching. Consider the following relationships:

  (1) Downstream: d = t₁ × (v + u)
  (2) Upstream: d = t₂ × (v – u)

Solving for v and u, we get:
  v + u = d / t₁
  v – u = d / t₂

Now, add the two equations:
  2v = (d / t₁) + (d / t₂)
  => v = [d / t₁ + d / t₂] / 2

Subtracting the upstream equation from the downstream equation:
  2u = (d / t₁) – (d / t₂)
  => u = [d / t₁ – d / t₂] / 2

Below is the optimal solution implemented in C++:

Copy
cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Given constants
    double d = 30.0;        // distance (km)
    double t_down = 3.0;    // time downstream (hours)
    double t_up = 5.0;      // time upstream (hours)
    
    // Calculate boat speed in still water (v) and stream speed (u)
    double v = (d / t_down + d / t_up) / 2.0;  // v = (d/t_down + d/t_up)/2
    double u = (d / t_down - d / t_up) / 2.0;    // u = (d/t_down - d/t_up)/2
    
    // Output the results
    cout << "Boat speed in still water (v): " << v << " km/h" << endl;
    cout << "Stream speed (u): " << u << " km/h" << endl;
    
    return 0;
}
Time Complexity: O(1) since the solution involves a constant number of arithmetic operations.
Space Complexity: O(1) as only a few variables are used.

